Z||000000||DXR_DAD||09-02-2001||01:31 AM||||Working on the Little red toy this weekend<BR>Seems as if it's getting too much fuel???<BR>plugs are soaked after the first few cranks<BR>with no start!!!!  I though I had bad injectors, I replaced them, New plugs, New coil, New dist cap, New fuel filter, and I'm getting spark! and there are no codes. Still the same problem!!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>This car was running fine when I got it about 3 months ago?? but It wasn't driven<BR>needed to get all the paper work done, fix a cracked manifold, and a few odds & ends,<P>What do I need to check???<P>Thanks<BR>DXR_DAD<P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Songman||09-02-2001||01:46 AM||||That is strange.. Gas and spark are the two things you have to have and you got those... Does it hit at all and then just not run, or does it not even hit?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif">  <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A><p>[This message has been edited by Songman (edited 09-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfiero||09-02-2001||01:49 AM||||How about the fuel pressure regulator?  They're cheap and reasonably easy to change (just need to remove the upper plenum).<P>It could also be your fuel pump. I had a similar problem with my '88 GT.  It would run and drive, but the fuel pump would (for lack of a better word) over pump when it got really  hot, like on a long drive.  I could release the built up pressure from the schrader valve and it would start right up (until it over pressurized again).<P>Theres my thoughts.......hopefully it's something simple. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||PBJ||09-02-2001||10:50 AM||||does it start when you have the gas pedal to the floor when cranking (clear flood mode). Possibly the coolant temp sensor has gone bad and the ecm is being told it's -35c.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/forsalestart.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||DXR_DAD||09-02-2001||01:55 PM||||Thanks guys! PBJ, I did think about that. It was talked about a few weeks ago, I pulled No codes!!!  mrfiero, from thte start it would not start, I did the same checked the schrader valve and had pressure? I changed the fuel rail and injectors?? samething???<P>Songman, it just turns over, but will not hit<P>MORE HELP NEEDED!!!!!<P>Thanks DXR_DAD||||reg||14
Z||000005||DXR_DAD||09-02-2001||02:24 PM||||Is that temp sensor the one close to the thermostat housing??? with the 1 connection plug???<P>Thanks<BR>DXR_DAD||||reg||5
Z||000006||Seanh||09-02-2001||02:45 PM||||Did you check compression maybe? If the ignition system is new, and your getting spark, and fuel obviously, then the only other thing I could think of would be compression. Also could be timing, if you had the distributor out to replace those things or somthing it might not be in the same way it came out (I know that can happen from experience, that problem kicked my ass for 2 days until I figured out what was wrong)||||reg||1
Z||000007||Songman||09-02-2001||02:55 PM||||I agree with, Sean.. Timing. If you have gas and spark, all you need is them aligned properly on compression for the thing to run, even if it runs poorly.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000008||beer_stud_21||09-02-2001||04:35 PM||||Yep, I would say timing too.  My car had sat for a few years prior to me buying it.  I had no idea what shape the timing was in so I re-timed in from scratch after I replaced the pickup coil.  What I did:  Pull the #1 plug.  Its the front right plug on the V6 when your standing at the trunk.  then lie on your back with the rear end of the car jacked up (and blocked up) and crank the engine over by hand with a deep socket and a big ratchet.  Have an assistant hold his finger over the plug hole until he feels the <B>compression</B> stroke.  Then keep cranking the engine over until it gets close to TDC.  use a <B>non breakable</B> rod to slide down the plug hole..  Use this to determine when you are at exactly TDC.  Then you gotta pull the distributor out and put it back in as to align the rotor to point exactly to the #1 plug wire on the dist cap.  It might take a few trys to get it exact, but thats fine as long as you dont crank the engine.  Once you got it lined up right leave the bolt that tightens the distributor down a bit loose.  You want to be able to rotate the dist by hand just a little bit.  Put the plug back in and have an assistant put his foot to the floor and crank while you slowly rotate the distributor as needed to get it to fire.  When your close it will sputter and you can keep going the same direction and it should run.  (I  believe rotating the dist clockwise will retard timing and counter clockwise will advance the timing.  But I could be wrong)  Once its running tighten down that bolt so nothing moves and you should be good to go.  You can check it with a timing light, but mine runs so good I havn't even bothered to do that.  <P>Note:  When you pull the distributor to align it with #1 plug wire it has to be just right.  I used a pencil or wood crayon to mark the plenum where the rotor was pointing when I have the dist cap off.  Then when you put the cap back on you can see exactly where the rotor is pointing and can rotate the body of the dist to match.  Just make sure you can still rotate the body of the dist quite a ways in both directions.  If you can't pull the dist again and move it over another notch or so.  This is important because if you dont do this when you start to crank you might get it to sputter and will be back at square one.  <P>Well, I hope I explained that well.  You probably already know how to do this, but if not ask me if you got a question.<P>-marlin<P>P.S:  Be sure to change the pickup coil when you pull the distributor.  They are less than $10.00 at Napa.  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||AL||09-03-2001||12:36 AM||||you changed cap,could it be that you didn't put the wires on right? when i did this, i had the same symptoms until i figured out that i didn't put the wires on correctly to the perpective plug.||||reg||1
Z||000010||DXR_DAD||09-03-2001||02:54 PM||||Thanks guys for all the info!!!! but remember this, the car was running fine????<BR>I drove it, parked it, went out the next day and it would not start!!!! and thats when I started to smell gas. Pulled the plugs and they were soaked. All the replaced parts, cap, plugs, coil, etc. were done after the problem started!!!! and it still will not start. I did a search and found that someone had the same problem, but no fix was ever posted?? I think it was key word: "Running to rich"<P>WHY WON'T IT START???  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Thanks<BR>DXR_DAD||||reg||5
Z||000011||Joe Torma||09-03-2001||05:00 PM||||Hook up a battery charger to your car full blast.  Hook up a timing light and hold the trigger while someone cranks the car over for 15seconds(don't kill the starter)...check for <u>consistant</u> spark.<P>This is the 6cyl right?<BR>How did you check for spark?<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/simplesig.gif">   <BR>Did you get your car in my book yet????<BR><A HREF="http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm</A> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 09-03-2001).]||||reg||1
